# Testing Standards

**Rewritten in P4.** The previous version was a generic JS/TS testing playbook —
Vitest, Jest, Mocha, React Testing Library, jsdom, `npm install`, and a
`package.json` test-script checklist. There is no JavaScript product surface in
this repo, which is why ESLint and JS unit tests are listed as accepted declines
in [`docs/Setup/DOCTOR_POLICY.md`](../../docs/Setup/DOCTOR_POLICY.md). It also
told the agent to set up a test framework, when a working one is already wired.

The test surface in this repo is three layers. Match the test to the layer.

| Layer | What it covers | How to run |
|---|---|---|
| **Python unit tests** | Pure logic: material rules, level-loader helpers, input assets, project setup | `Content/Python/tests/test_*.py`, auto-discovered by the `PythonAutomationTest` plugin — Editor → Tools → Test Automation |
| **PIE validation** | Does it actually work in a running game: character spawn, ground contact, animation, PCG | `execute_python_script("pie_test_runner.py")` via MCP, then read `Saved/pie_test_results.json` |
| **C++ automation** | Engine-side behaviour | `Source/HomeWorld/`, `Source/HomeWorldEditor/`; run under the Automation system |

A logic bug is a unit-test failure. A "it compiles but nothing happens in PIE"
bug is a PIE-runner result, not something a unit test can reach.

## Editor tests are flaky by nature

Level load plus World Partition streaming makes Editor tests non-deterministic.
The project already has the mitigations: a smart level loader and latent tests.
See [`docs/Testing/LEVEL_TESTING_PLAN.md`](../../docs/Testing/LEVEL_TESTING_PLAN.md).

- Do not "fix" a flaky Editor test by loosening the assertion. Find out what
  state the test assumed and did not establish.
- Assert on things you set up in the test, not on global Editor state left over
  from the previous test.
- Log the precondition you depend on. When a test fails only sometimes, the
  missing log line is the bug.

## Writing a test

- **Name it for the behaviour:** `test_gives_nurtured_mesh_when_herb_is_gathered`
  beats `test_gather_2`. The name is the documentation.
- **Cover the error path, not just the happy one.** Most real bugs in this repo
  are the missing property, the absent asset, the deferred `destroy_actor`.
- **Cover the edges:** empty, missing, and boundary values.
- **Clean up what you create** — an Editor test that leaves an actor behind
  breaks the test after it. This is the same check-before-create rule as
  standard practice, applied to teardown.
- **Keep unit tests fast and pure.** If a test needs the Editor running, it
  belongs in the PIE layer instead.

## Before claiming a fix works

A test that was never run proves nothing. Run the suite, and for anything
touching the Editor or C++, run `.\Tools\Safe-Build.ps1` and a PIE pass — then
quote the actual result. Unverified is not verified; see
`verification-evidence` and [`docs/KNOWN_ERRORS.md`](../../docs/KNOWN_ERRORS.md)
for the project's standard on evidence.

## Checklist

- [ ] Test lives in the layer that can actually reach the behaviour
- [ ] Name states the expected behaviour
- [ ] Error and edge cases covered, not just the happy path
- [ ] Cleans up everything it creates
- [ ] Suite actually run, and the result quoted — not assumed
